WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:06.820
Well, each one of us had to live half a term in the home management house.

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.300
So when you were a home economics major, you had to learn to manage a home.

3
00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:18.360
That’s what home economics was . . . the money management in the home is what it really was,

4
00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:25.660
so therefore, you had to learn about food preparation, [which] was a big part of the expense of the family.

5
00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.480
And you realize coming from the Depression times then this was extremely important.

6
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:40.020
And that’s when home economics developed, was to be able to make the most of family resources no matter what they were.

7
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:46.800
In our classes, we were taught how to make furniture out of orange crates.

8
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:58.300
We were taught how to take men’s worn-out trousers that were worn in the seat and to make a skirt out of the legs that were not worn.

9
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:03.060
I remember we took some old wicker furniture and we upholstered [it].

10
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:15.540
We padded it with cotton and put slipcovers on it so it looked like a . . . We were taught to make do with whatever we had.

11
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:20.120
One of the professors went to [Louisiana] Tech and majored in home ec;

12
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:26.820
she made all of her clothes out of flour sacks because they really didn’t have any money.

13
00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:33.660
They looked like . . . she got the solid colors so they looked like linen, and made some really very beautiful clothes.

14
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:40.920
She taught clothing construction later at LSU from her beginnings of flour sacks.
